<p>Holding off a vote until I can read this fully, but I can tell you that you know at least a little of what you're doing by the first part of the screenplay. My father and I are both in the film industry and this, on first glance, is fairly competent. 56 pages is 56 minutes, going by the one-page-is-one-minute rule, so you're good on that aspect. That said, in reading the first part of the script you seem to have a lot of camera angles detailed; that's not necessary. This is a narrative script, and camera details can be left to the camera script. Generally camera angles are only included/described in the main script if they're absolutely key to conveying the layout of the scene.</p>
<p>Edit: I am a little worried that the writing style of a screenplay isn't gonna fly on this site. If I didn't have experience with film and I saw the weird overly-expository tone screenplays have without the prior context of knowing that, yes, screenplays really <em>are</em> written that casually/second-person-y, I'd think it was just lazy writing and not an industry-specific writing style.</p>

