															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/randomini" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1379080); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1379080&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606230085" alt="Randomini" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1379080)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/randomini" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1379080); return false;" >Randomini</a></span> <span class="odate time_1476254425 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">12 Oct 2016 06:40</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2669098">
									

<p>Based on comments upthread, people seem to think this is a lot larger than it is. It's about 12.5k words, which really doesn't take that long to read. Shorter works have been posted without the disclaimers that you've included.</p>
<p>On to critique. Note that all of this should be taken with the caveat that I'm not a screenwriter, and I don't read too many screenplays - but I think the same is true of anyone reading this.</p>
<p>Firstly, this is badly formatted for the site (which you already knew). I copy-pasted the page source into Word, and I'd recommend anyone who wants to read it in full to do the same, since it retains the original indenting.</p>
<p>Next, the dialogue. It's not great. This is admittedly the problem in writing a pilot intended for a general audience; you get infodumps like:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Let's just say that the Foundation isn't the only game in town when it comes to the anomalous world. There are many Groups of Interest competing with us.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>There are better ways to do this: what you've done is telling, not showing, and the primary strength of the audiovisual medium is that you can literally show people things you want them to understand. Also, the number of times that you have ground-level personnel scripted to say &quot;SCP-140&quot; in full earlier on when they'd probably be calling it &quot;140&quot; for urgency's sake is a bit confusing. You stop doing it later, but you should probably do it from the start, apart from Mahto as the newbie. I'd also recommend not using digits in the dialogue - characters might be saying &quot;one hundred and fourty&quot; or &quot;one fourty&quot; or &quot;one four zero&quot;, and it should be unambiguous to an actor reading the script. You could actually vary it up, having each character pronounce it consistently but differently to the other characters. Another example of where show, don't tell would improve this work is in Jafari's various lines:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>My life has been about studying the Daevas<br />
I just do not feel I can miss this opportunity.<br />
The Daevas have been my life's work<br />
What? No! All our work!</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This feels weak and cliche. Another instance:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I guess that old saying is right, then.<br />
Which one is that?<br />
The truth hurts.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, very cliche, kind of weak dialogue. I can see how this MIGHT be well-executed by the right actors, but the words by themselves aren't great, and that's what I have to judge on here.</p>
<p>Plot: pretty slow by words, but that's often how television works. Pretty boring, but that's often how pilots work. I'm not going to get into nitpicking details on this one, but it's not engaging to me. If this narrative was written as a tale, I don't think it would work for many people at all.</p>
<p>This is probably because the characters aren't especially intriguing, and that's where this really dies for me. Your disclaimer at the beginning refers to the script being &quot;Whedonesque&quot;. Your dialogue certainly hits close to the same notes as Whedon's does. The problem is that Whedon's works lean heavily on interesting characters, who might start as cliches but build upon that to construct (or at least allude to) intriguing and deep backgrounds. Take a line of Whedon dialogue, and you probably know the character that spoke it. Not so much in this script.</p>
<p>Consider the pilot episode of Firefly: a whole bunch of in-medias-res, no explaining to the viewer what's going on. The fact that the characters don't comment on much in particular indicates that this is a run of the mill day. As far as I can recall, that episode doesn't really have an infodump moment at all: you're just shown things, people, relationships, their actions, and Whedon assumes that you as the viewer are able to get the gist of how this setting works. This entire script, to me, feels like a big long infodump, and your characters feel too generic for me to care about any of them. Again, it's possible this is something the right crew of actors would solve, or further episodes would elaborate on - but I'm reading this as a stand-alone screenplay, and assessing it as such.</p>
<p>All up, I didn't like much of what I saw. Props for trying what you did, and it's a solid first try, but I feel this needs a hell of a lot more revision to work.</p>

							</div>

								
					
