														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2669200">
									

<p>Randomini has more or less nailed the key issues here. For a script (and I've read quite a few, although mostly film scripts) this does a huge amount of info-dumping, rather than showing. The result is that it sounds like a B-movie, which I don't think is what you intend. Starting in media res with a more &quot;typical Foundation day&quot; scenario might work, as Randomini suggests. Another approach would be working from the perspective of Dr Mahto, rather than Agent Grey - as Dr Mahto is a neophyte, the audience can learn the Foundation's workings at the same pace as she does (as in the film Hellboy).</p>
<p>More fundamentally, I'm not sure I can get behind this version of the Foundation for TV. You're pitching it like &quot;Agents of Shield&quot; in tone, when I'd naturally lean towards &quot;X-Files&quot;. I get that you're after Whedon-esque, but then I'd say &quot;Dollhouse&quot; - make the whole concept of the Foundation uncomfortable for the viewer, and while you can have sequences of action or suspense, don't solve all your problems with fists. As soon as the first aikido fight started, I checked out.</p>
<p>Incidentally, I would recommend reading scripts to casual site users who haven't done so. There are a number of sites out there with free scripts available, and while many scripts are terrible, the good ones are very good, and almost instantly recognisable as good.</p>

